\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces High-level architecture of the proposed framework. The system consists of a modality routing model, specialized disease classifiers, Explainable AI modules, and a Federated Learning infrastructure connecting multiple healthcare institutions.}}{64}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Classification tree of the proposed two-level framework. The routing model first determines the image modality before directing the image toward the appropriate disease-specific classifier.}}{66}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Two-row architecture of the DenseNet121-based medical image routing model.}}{73}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Architectures and workflow investigated during the implementation of the routing model. Blue blocks represent inputs, green blocks denote processing stages, and orange blocks correspond to classification outputs.}}{76}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Overview of the DenseNet121 architecture showing dense blocks, transition layers, and the final classification stage.}}{77}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Overall architecture of the proposed chest disease multi-class classification system, illustrating the sequential pipeline from raw CXR acquisition through preprocessing, lung segmentation, deep classification, and visual explainability.}}{82}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Representative chest radiograph samples from each of the four target diagnostic classes. The subtle visual differences between COVID-19 and Pneumonia are clear, underscoring the difficulty of the classification task.}}{85}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Comparison of a Pneumonia chest radiograph before (left) and after (right).}}{87}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Comparison of a Tuberculosis chest radiograph before (left) and after (right) the preprocessing pipeline.}}{87}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Representative image–mask pair from the Darwin lung segmentation dataset. The mask precisely delineates both lung fields including regions behind the cardiac shadow.}}{88}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces U-Net architecture implemented for lung segmentation. The contracting path (left) captures contextual information through successive down sampling, while the expansive path (right) enables precise localization via skip connections. Tensor shapes are annotated at each level.}}{91}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces U-Net segmentation results. From left to right: the preprocessed input radiograph, the predicted binary lung mask, and the masked output image passed to the classification stage.}}{94}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Proposed DenseNet121 OVR classification model architecture. The base network produces a 1024-dimensional feature vector via Global Average Pooling, which is refined through the custom classification head before producing independent sigmoid probability scores for each diagnostic class.}}{96}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM visualization results for Pneumonia representative case. The saliency maps confirm that the model attends to pathologically relevant lung regions.}}{99}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM visualization results for Tuberculosis cases. The upper-lower lobe localization of saliency for TB cases confirms the model's ability to recognize TB-specific radiological patterns.}}{99}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces YOLOv8 architecture pipeline for automated localization and classification of pulmonary abnormalities.}}{101}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Qualitative evaluation showing tumor localization (confidence scores 0.72 and 0.60) on an axial CT slice.}}{103}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces Qualitative evaluation showing tumor localization (confidence score 0.87) on an axial CT slice.}}{103}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces Example of an interpretable YOLO detection result on a chest CT image. The model localizes a suspected lesion using a bounding box and reports a confidence score of 0.87. Such spatial predictions provide inherent interpretability by explicitly identifying the anatomical region associated with the model's decision, reducing the need for post-hoc visualization techniques.}}{104}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces Detailed architectural diagram of the YOLOv8 object detection framework, illustrating the Backbone, Neck (FPN + PAN), and Detection Head stages for multi-scale prediction.}}{106}{figure.caption.66}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.21}{\ignorespaces Two-tier federated learning system architecture. Hospital clients communicate with the central federated learning server through TLS-secured gRPC channels, while the REST management layer provides round lifecycle management and monitoring. Raw medical data remains within each hospital boundary.}}{111}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces Privacy--utility tradeoff under $(\epsilon ,\delta )$-differential privacy. Smaller values of $\epsilon $ provide stronger privacy guarantees but may reduce model performance.}}{116}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.23}{\ignorespaces Overall architecture of the proposed Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) model. The user query is transformed into vector embeddings, relevant medical knowledge is retrieved from the FAISS vector database, and the retrieved context is supplied to the Qwen 2.5 language model to generate a grounded and source-aware response.}}{121}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.24}{\ignorespaces Query processing workflow of the proposed RAG system.}}{123}{figure.caption.74}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces End-to-end logical architecture of the proposed X-Fed NeuroScan framework, illustrating the interaction between users, backend services, AI models, explainability components, federated learning infrastructure, and data storage systems.}}{127}{figure.caption.78}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces Deployment architecture of the proposed system showing the frontend application, backend services, model-serving infrastructure, federated learning server, databases, storage components, and external integrations.}}{128}{figure.caption.79}%
